      lesson: `### Start with the question the flow meter answers

A flow meter answers one question: how much water passes a point per unit time? Operators then need to translate that number into pipe velocity, tank filling time or plant throughput, and across the US and Canada the same flow is quoted in several units. This module gives you one relationship, **Q = A x V**, and a short list of conversions that cover almost every exam problem.

> **Your job:** keep the units consistent. Most wrong answers in flow problems come from mixing feet, inches, gallons and minutes in one line of arithmetic.

### The flow equation

For water filling a pipe or channel, the flow rate equals the cross-sectional area of the flowing water multiplied by its average velocity:

**Q = A x V**

- Q is flow in cubic feet per second (cfs) when A is in square feet and V is in feet per second (ft/s).
- A is the area at right angles to the direction of flow.
- V is the average velocity. (1 ft/s = 0.305 m/s.)

Rearranged, **V = Q / A** and **A = Q / V**. Because A sits in the denominator of the velocity equation, the same flow moves *faster* through a smaller area. That single fact explains why a partly closed valve jets water, why a small service line is noisy, and why velocity rises in a reducer.

### Areas you need

**Circular pipe** flowing full:

**A = pi x d squared / 4**, or equivalently pi x r squared, where r is the radius, half the diameter.

Diameters are quoted in inches but the equation needs feet, so divide by 12 first. (1 in = 25.4 mm.) For an 8 in pipe, d = 8 / 12 = 0.667 ft and A = 3.1416 x 0.667 squared / 4 = **0.349 square feet**.

Two classic errors: using the diameter where the formula wants the radius, which overstates area four-fold, and forgetting to square.

**Rectangular channel**:

**A = width x depth of water**

Use the depth of the *water*, not the depth of the channel wall. A 3 ft wide channel with water 1.5 ft deep has A = 4.5 square feet.

### The conversions

Operators move between four common flow units. Learn the constants and what each one links.

Gallons in this course are US gallons (an Imperial gallon is about 1.2 US gallons).

| Constant | Meaning | Metric equivalent |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 7.48 gal per cubic foot | volume | 1 ft3 = 28.3 L |
| 449 gpm per cfs | flow, gallons per minute and cubic feet per second | 1 cfs = 28.3 L/s; 1 gpm = 0.0631 L/s |
| 1.547 cfs per MGD | flow, million gallons per day and cfs | 1 MGD = 43.8 L/s |
| 694.4 gpm per MGD | 1,000,000 gal per day divided by 1,440 min per day | 1 MGD = 43.8 L/s |

Where do they come from? 1 cfs is 7.48 gal each second, and there are 60 seconds in a minute, so 7.48 x 60 = 448.8, rounded to 449 gpm. One MGD is 1,000,000 gal per day, which is 1,000,000 / 1,440 = 694.4 gpm, and 694.4 / 449 = 1.547 cfs. Knowing the derivation helps you rebuild a constant if you forget one.

The sense-check for direction: a cfs is a large unit (449 gpm), so converting gpm to cfs makes the number smaller, and converting cfs to gpm makes it bigger. MGD to gpm makes the number much bigger.

### Continuity: the same flow, different pipe sizes

For steady flow with nothing added or removed, the flow is the same at every section of a pipe. So

**Q = A1 x V1 = A2 x V2**

and therefore **V2 = V1 x (A1 / A2)**.

Because area depends on the *square* of the diameter, halving a pipe's diameter makes the area one-quarter and the velocity four times higher. Doubling the diameter drops the velocity to one-quarter. Velocity does not change in proportion to diameter; it changes inversely with the square of the diameter: V2 = V1 x (d1 / d2) squared. That is the most tested continuity idea.

<figure>
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 380 200" role="img" aria-label="A pipe narrows from a large diameter to a small one. A short arrow in the wide section and a long arrow in the narrow section show velocity is higher where the pipe is smaller. Flow Q = A times V is equal at both sections." font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#1d2d40">
<title>Continuity in a reducer</title>
<path d="M20 50H150L210 75H360V105H210L150 130H20Z" fill="#e3edf8" stroke="#6b7a8c" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="20" y="40">Large area A1, slow V1</text><text x="210" y="65">Small area A2, fast V2</text><path d="M35 90H70" stroke="#2f6db2" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M75 90l-8 -4v8z" fill="#2f6db2"/><text x="85" y="95">short = slow</text><path d="M225 90H340" stroke="#2f6db2" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M345 90l-8 -4v8z" fill="#2f6db2"/><text x="285" y="124" text-anchor="middle">long = fast</text><text x="190" y="170" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">Q = A x V is the same at both sections</text><text x="190" y="188" text-anchor="middle">A1 x V1 = A2 x V2</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>The same flow passes both sections, so the smaller area must carry faster water, shown by the longer arrow.</figcaption>
</figure>


Higher velocity matters for operations: friction losses climb (Module 3), surge pressures from a sudden stop are larger (Module 4), and scour or noise can develop in fittings.

### Worked example: a transfer main with a reducer

*The Brightwater Bluffs booster station is fictional. Use 7.48 gal per ft3, 449 gpm per cfs.*

A 10 in main carries 700 gpm and then narrows to a 6 in line.

**Step 1: convert the flow to cfs.** 700 gpm / 449 gpm per cfs = **1.559 cfs**.

**Step 2: area of the 10 in main.** d = 10 / 12 = 0.8333 ft. A = 3.1416 x 0.8333 squared / 4 = **0.5454 ft2**.

**Step 3: velocity in the 10 in main.** V = Q / A = 1.559 / 0.5454 = **2.86 ft/s** (0.87 m/s).

**Step 4: area of the 6 in line.** d = 0.5 ft, A = 3.1416 x 0.5 squared / 4 = **0.1963 ft2**.

**Step 5: velocity in the 6 in line.** V = 1.559 / 0.1963 = **7.94 ft/s** (2.42 m/s).

**Sense-check by ratio.** The area ratio is (10 / 6) squared = 2.78, and 2.86 x 2.78 = 7.95 ft/s, which matches 7.94 ft/s within rounding. The calculation agrees. Notice that the diameter ratio alone (1.67) would have given only 4.8 ft/s, which is the typical shortcut error.

### A second example: from MGD to channel velocity

A plant effluent channel is 4 ft wide with water 2 ft deep. The plant is passing 3.0 MGD. Convert: 3.0 MGD x 1.547 = 4.64 cfs. Area = 4 x 2 = 8 ft2. Velocity = 4.64 / 8 = **0.58 ft/s**. A low velocity such as this is typical of a settling or effluent channel. If the same flow were forced through a half-depth channel the velocity would double, because the area halved.

### Common mistakes to watch for

- **Diameter in inches in the area formula.** Convert to feet first, or your area will be in square inches and 144 times too large when mixed with cfs.
- **Radius versus diameter.** Check which one the formula wants. Pi x d squared / 4 uses diameter; pi x r squared uses radius.
- **Forgetting to convert gpm to cfs.** If the answer is in the thousands of ft/s, a conversion step was skipped.
- **Dividing when you should multiply.** Remember cfs are bigger units than gpm. Use the direction check.
- **Comparing diameters rather than areas.** Velocity scales with the square of the diameter ratio (larger pipe, slower water).
- **Using channel depth rather than water depth.** Area is the wetted section only.

### Before you move on

You should now be able to compute the area of a circular pipe or rectangular channel, apply Q = A x V in either direction, convert among gpm, cfs and MGD using 449, 7.48 and 1.547, and predict qualitatively how velocity changes when pipe size changes. Module 3 asks what that velocity costs in energy.`,
