      id: "characteristics-and-train",
      title: "Wastewater characteristics and the treatment train",
      objectives: [
        "Describe what arrives at a wastewater plant and how the main measures of strength are used.",
        "Explain how flow changes and the start of the treatment train affect what the plant must handle."
      ],
      lesson: `### What arrives at the plant

Domestic wastewater is more than 99 percent water. The rest is dissolved and suspended material from homes, businesses and industry, plus whatever runs into the sewers from streets and roofs. The plant's job is to take that material out of the water in stages, so that the effluent leaving the plant does not harm the receiving water. Understanding the influent is the first step, because every process after it is sized and run for the load it receives.

### Strength and the main measures

The strength of wastewater describes how much pollution it carries. Biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) measures the oxygen that microbes use to break down organic matter in a sample over a set time. Total suspended solids (TSS) measure the particles that a filter retains. Nutrients such as nitrogen and phosphorus are measured separately because they matter for biological treatment and for the receiving water. A plant that reports strength in several measures can see what kind of load it is dealing with, not only how much.

<figure>
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 390 260" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: in dry weather flow is lower and strength higher; in storm flow is higher and strength lower, with a similar load" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#1d2d40">
<title>Dilution: the storm raises the flow and lowers the strength, so the load can stay about the same.</title>
<text x="110" y="24" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">Dry weather</text><text x="290" y="24" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">Storm</text><rect x="40" y="60" width="50" height="140" fill="#2c6e9a"/><rect x="100" y="110" width="50" height="90" fill="#9fb0bf"/><rect x="220" y="90" width="50" height="110" fill="#2c6e9a"/><rect x="280" y="60" width="50" height="140" fill="#9fb0bf"/><text x="65" y="216" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Flow</text><text x="125" y="216" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Strength</text><text x="245" y="216" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Flow</text><text x="305" y="216" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Strength</text><text x="195" y="250" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6b7a8c">Illustrative, relative units. Flow and strength trade off, so the load stays about the same.</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>Dilution: the storm raises the flow and lowers the strength, so the load can stay about the same.</figcaption>
</figure>

### Flow changes and why they matter

Wastewater flow changes through the day, with peaks in the morning and evening. Rain changes it more. Infiltration and inflow are groundwater and stormwater that enter the collection system through cracks, joints and connections. They raise the flow and dilute the wastewater, so the concentration of pollutants falls even though the total load may rise. Peak flows can exceed the hydraulic capacity of a unit and carry solids over if the plant is not prepared for them.

### Where the treatment train starts

A typical train begins with preliminary treatment: screens to remove rags and debris, and grit chambers to remove sand and gravel. These steps protect pumps and equipment downstream. Primary clarifiers then settle the heavier solids, which become primary sludge. Biological treatment, usually an activated sludge process, follows, and disinfection and effluent handling come at the end. Each step depends on the one before it, so changes at the start of the train affect everything after.

### What operators watch first

Operators watch flow, influent BOD and TSS, and the condition of the screens and grit removal at the start of the plant. A rising flow with steady strength suggests dilution. A falling flow with rising strength can suggest a blockage or a sewer problem upstream. Trends across several days tell more than any single sample.`,
      evidence: "### Fictional scenario: Pinecrest Plant after the storm\n\nPinecrest Wastewater Plant is a fictional plant. After a night of heavy rain, the influent flow doubled and the BOD of the influent fell by about a third. The screens caught more debris than usual, and the grit chamber collected a large amount of sand. The operator asks what the rain has done to the load and what needs watching over the next two days.",
      assignment: "Write a one-page note for Pinecrest's supervisor. Explain why the influent BOD fell while the total load may have stayed about the same. Describe the effect of the extra debris and grit on the screens and the grit chamber. List the measurements you would track over the next two days, with the reason for each. Name anything you could not tell from the scenario.",
